Linux has file permissions
read r 4
write w 2
excute x 1

linux also has special permissions 
SUID           Run program as file owner
SGID           Inherit group / run with group permissions
Sticky Bit     Prevent users from deleting others files

SUID -  when a user runs a file, the file runs with owners permission not the users permission

For example if users excute the passwd command 

while executing the file users will have temporary owner permissions to write the password to file while executing 

normal users cannot write the file directly 

this is possible with SUID
chmod 4755 file name here 4 is for SUID

SGID
it works differently for both files and directories

if an user the excute the files the file runs with group's privileges 

if a file is created inside a directory, file is created with users group not the directory ' s group 

with SGID enabled for a directory the file created inside the directory will be created with the directory 's group
chmod 2755 file_name 2 is for SGID

sticky bit
in a directory the with write permission enabled, any user can delete any user's files
with sticky bit enabled only users can delete their files not others files
